  "body": "## Current Behavior\n\nWhen calling `POST /cluster/recover` on a Qdrant instance running in **standalone** (non-distributed) mode, the API returns HTTP 500 Internal Server Error. This is incorrect — the server is not experiencing an internal error; it's running in a mode where the requested operation is not applicable.\n\n```bash\ncurl -X POST \"http://localhost:6333/cluster/recover\"\n# → HTTP 500 ❌ (WRONG — should be 4xx)\n# {\"status\":{\"error\":\"Service internal error: Qdrant is running in standalone mode\"}}\n```\n\nThe error message body is correct in describing the situation (\"Qdrant is running in standalone mode\"), but the **HTTP status code is wrong**. A 500-series status code indicates a server-side failure, which is misleading for an operation that simply doesn't apply in the current mode.\n\n## Steps to Reproduce\n\n1. Start Qdrant v1.18.2 in standalone mode (default):\n   ```bash\n   docker run -p 6333:6333 qdrant/qdrant:v1.18.2\n   ```\n\n2. Call the cluster recover endpoint:\n   ```bash\n   curl -X POST \"http://localhost:6333/cluster/recover\" -i\n   # → HTTP/1.1 500 Internal Server Error\n   # {\"status\":{\"error\":\"Service internal error: Qdrant is running in standalone mode\"},\"time\":0.000135}\n   ```\n\n3. Verify the instance is not distributed:\n   ```bash\n   curl \"http://localhost:6333/cluster\"\n   # → {\"result\":{\"status\":\"disabled\"},\"status\":\"ok\",\"time\":0.0}\n   ```\n\n### MRE Script\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"MRE: POST /cluster/recover returns 500 in standalone mode on Qdrant v1.18.2\"\"\"\nimport requests\n\nBASE = \"http://localhost:6333\"\n\nr = requests.post(f\"{BASE}/cluster/recover\", timeout=10)\nprint(f\"POST /cluster/recover: status={r.status_code}\")\nprint(f\"Body: {r.text}\")\n\nassert r.status_code == 500, \\\n    f\"Expected 500 (defect), got {r.status_code}\"\n\n# The error message is correct but the status code is wrong\nbody = r.json()\nerror = body.get(\"status\", {}).get(\"error\", \"\")\nassert \"standalone mode\" in error\n\nprint(f\"\\nDEFECT CONFIRMED: 500 Internal Server Error for non-applicable operation\")\nprint(f\"Expected: 4xx (400 Bad Request or 405 Method Not Allowed)\")\nprint(f\"Actual: 500 (implies server crash/bug, which is misleading)\")\nprint(f\"Error message: {error}\")\nprint(f\"\\nNote: The error message is correct — the status code is wrong.\")\n```\n\n## Expected Behavior\n\n`POST /cluster/recover` in standalone mode should return **HTTP 400 Bad Request** (or HTTP 405 Method Not Allowed), not HTTP 500:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"status\": {\n    \"error\": \"Bad request: Cluster operations are not available — Qdrant is running in standalone mode\"\n  },\n  \"time\": 0.0\n}\n```\n\n**Rationale**:\n- **400 Bad Request**: The operation cannot be performed because the server is in standalone mode — this is a client-side error (requesting an unsupported operation), not a server error\n- **405 Method Not Allowed**: The `/cluster/recover` endpoint is not available in standalone mode — the method is known but not supported in this configuration\n\nHTTP 500 is reserved for unexpected server failures (bugs, crashes, resource exhaustion). A predictable configuration state (standalone mode) that prevents an operation should return a 4xx status.\n\n## Possible Solution\n\nIn the cluster recover handler, check the cluster mode before returning an error:\n\n```rust\n// Current (suspected):\n// Cluster operations check mode, and if not distributed,\n// return an error with status code 500\n\n// Fix: change the status code to 400\nfn handle_cluster_recover(state: &State) -> Result<Response, Status> {\n    if state.is_standalone() {\n        return Err(Status::new(\n            400, // ← was 500\n            \"Cluster operations are not available: Qdrant is running in standalone mode\"\n        ));\n    }\n    // ... proceed with recovery\n}\n```\n\nA one-line status code change is sufficient to fix this issue.\n\n## Context (Environment)\n\n- **Qdrant version**: v1.18.2 (single node, Docker)\n- **API**: REST, `POST /cluster/recover`\n- **Documentation**: https://api.qdrant.tech/v-1-18-x/api-reference\n- **Doc states**: \"Attempts recovery of current peer Raft state\" with \"200 on success\" — no mention of standalone mode behavior\n- **Cluster status**: `GET /cluster` returns `{\"result\":{\"status\":\"disabled\"},\"status\":\"ok\"}`\n- **Discovery method**: Automated contract-based fuzzing + independent manual reproduction\n- **Related**: [#9017](https://github.com/qdrant/qdrant/issues/9017) (hnsw_ef accepts 0 — fixed via PR #9320, demonstrates maintainer willingness to fix validation issues)\n\n## Detailed Description\n\nHTTP status codes have well-defined semantics per [RFC 7231](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc7231):\n\n- **4xx (Client Error)**: The request contains bad syntax or cannot be fulfilled — the client should modify the request\n- **5xx (Server Error)**: The server failed to fulfill an apparently valid request — indicates a server-side problem\n\nCalling a cluster-only endpoint on a standalone instance is a **client error** (the client should not make this request to this server). Returning 500 misleads monitoring systems, log analyzers, and developers into thinking a server crash occurred.\n\nThe error message body is already correct and helpful:\n```\n\"Service internal error: Qdrant is running in standalone mode\"\n```\n\nThe only fix needed is changing \"Service internal error\" to an appropriate 4xx class and removing the word \"internal\" from the message:\n```\n\"Bad request: Cluster operations are not available — Qdrant is running in standalone mode\"\n```\n\n**Consistency with other cluster endpoints**: It's worth checking whether other `/cluster/*` endpoints (e.g., `DELETE /cluster/peer/{peer_id}`) exhibit the same 500-instead-of-4xx behavior in standalone mode. The same root cause may affect the entire cluster API surface.\n\n## Possible Implementation\n\n1. Change the error status code from 500 to 400 in the cluster recover handler\n2. Audit all `/cluster/*` endpoints for the same pattern:\n   ```bash\n   # Check each cluster endpoint in standalone mode\n   curl -X DELETE \"http://localhost:6333/cluster/peer/1\"\n   curl -X POST \"http://localhost:6333/cluster/recover\"\n   ```\n3. Update the error message prefix from \"Service internal error\" to \"Bad request\" for these cases\n4. Add an integration test:\n   ```python\n   def test_cluster_recover_standalone_returns_4xx():\n       r = requests.post(f\"{BASE}/cluster/recover\")\n       assert r.status_code == 400  # not 500\n       assert \"standalone mode\" in r.text\n   ```\n5. Document the standalone mode behavior in the API reference for cluster endpoints\n",
